It's amazing how often one gets caught by tunnel
vision in NetHack.

You want to train the pet?

You don't have to do that from the hall, where
there's only one space "next to you", or from the
doorway, where there's at most three.

Walk around the shop. In the center, you have eight
squares around you on which the pet can drop
something, so your odds go up, and your odds of
being able to toss a treat to the pet after the item
is dropped goes up.

[So far as I can remember, from source code I read
a few years ago, what matters isn't rewarding a pet
which is standing on a dropped item next to you.
What matters is rewarding it in the very next turn
after it drops the item, which you can do if, after
dropping the item, the pet is on any of the many
squares around you to which you can throw along a
diagonal, horizontal, or vertical.]

The first time the pet drops something next to you,
reward it. With luck, and good preparation by you,
that's the only thing the pet has available to pick
up, so now, with that item in your grasp, you're
ready to drop money and have the pet drag it too
you.

Another note, in response to something Topi said.

When you stack stuff up in one big pile, and don't
want the pet diverted by picking stuff up from the
stack, it isn't sufficient to have something cursed
"in the stack", you need to have it on top of the
stack.

Many times I've watched a pet step "reluctantly"
onto a stack of goods, then gleefully pick up
something uncursed from the top and dance
around unable to pick up gold.

But if the top thing in the stack is a cursed item,
the pet absolutely (IIUC) refuses to pick it up.

